notes: |
  Slide 11: The Next Gravitational Wave Era: From Ground Upgrades to Space Observatories.
  - Summarizes the three-stage roadmap for the next two decades of gravitational-wave astrophysics:
    1. Near-term terrestrial upgrades (2026-2029): Advanced LIGO A+ and Advanced Virgo post-O4, incorporating heavy test masses, higher laser power, and balanced filter cavity squeezing to reach BNS ranges of ~250-300 Mpc.
    2. Third-generation (3G) ground observatories (2035+): Cosmic Explorer (USA, 40 km L-shaped surface arms) and Einstein Telescope (Europe, 10 km subterranean equilateral triangle with cryogenic optics). Will achieve redshift reach z > 10, detecting every stellar-mass black hole merger in the observable universe (~10^5 events/year).
    3. Space-borne millihertz observatory (2035+): ESA/NASA LISA (2.5 million km arm laser interferometer in heliocentric orbit), probing supermassive black hole mergers (10^5 to 10^7 M_sun) and extreme mass-ratio inspirals (EMRIs).
  - Concludes with the ultimate takeaway: gravitational-wave astronomy will map compact object coalescences across all of cosmic time back to the first stars.
